   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the correction law and the executive law, in relation to\nproviding for the return of any identification materials to an individual upon\nrelease from custody",
    "basePrintNo": "S5092",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5092-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §125, Cor L; add §837-x, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S5092\n\nSPONSOR: CLEARE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the correction law and the executive law, in relation to\nproviding for the return of any identification materials to an individ-\nual upon release from custody\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo prevent law enforcement from exacting a punishment on the detained\nbeyond their mandate of detaining or incarcerating individuals.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends subdivision 1 of section 125 of the corrections law to\nadd identification materials to the detained person's items that shall\nbe shall be kept by a correctional superintendent and returned upon\ndischarge.\n\n§ 2 Creates a new section 837-w of the executive law to require that law\nenforcement officials shall return identification materials to persons\nbeing released from custody.\n\n§ 3 Sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn some circumstances, law enforcement and corrections officials fail to\nprovide persons leaving their jurisdiction with their identifying mate-\nrials upon releasing them from custody. This can produce unnecessary\nhardship for persons being released, as any person needs proper iden-\ntification materials to perform the most ordinary day-to-day actions,\nlike opening a bank account, entering a government building, or even\njoining a gym. Whether the failure to provide individuals with their\nidentification materials is intentional or inadvertent, it imposes a\nhardship upon persons that is not prescribed by law. This bill explicit-\nly obligates law enforcement and corrections to avoid such action.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect ittm-ediately.\n",
    "multiSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
